tage zero is `[]` which `nil_mem_fTok` puts into `fTok`, and a successor stage lies in `fTok` of the preceding components, which sit inside the two colimits, so `fTok_mono` places it in `fTok Γ_D Γ_E`. `gTok_GammaD_GammaE` is the same argument with `gTok` and `mem_gTok_iUnion`. `exists_double_fixedPoint` packages `[] ∈ Γ_D`, `[] ∈ Γ_E`, and the two token equations. `master_Fsol` and `master_Gsol` are the definitional identities of the masters of `Fsol Dsol Esol` and `Gsol Dsol Esol` with `fTok Γ_D Γ_E` and `gTok Γ_D Γ_E`. `Dsol` and `Esol` are the singleton systems on those token sets. `Dsol_subsystem` rewrites the master of `Fsol Dsol Esol` to `Γ_D` by `master_Fsol.trans fTok_GammaD_GammaE`, observes that the only neighbourhood of `Dsol` is that master, and checks the intersection clause by `X = Y = Γ_D`; `Esol_subsystem` is the same calculation for `Gsol`. `exists_simultaneous_subsystems`, which is the numbered theorem `example_624`, is the conjunction of those two subsystem facts. The exercise asks for the two congruences. They fail for these systems: `sumTok` admits every left copy `embBit false X` with `X` a neighbourhood of the left summand, `Γ_D` is a neighbourhood of `Dsol`, and `embF_ne_sumTokMaster` says no false-tagged set equals the sum master because `[]` lies in `sumTokMaster` and in no `embBit false` set, so `0·Γ_D` is a neighbourhood of `Dsol.sum (Dsol.prod Esol)` and is not a neighbourhood of `Dsol`. The one-point systems are proper subsystems. The parenthetical about Exercise 6.23 has no formal counterpart. **Theorem:** partial — `exists_double_fixedPoint` solves the coupled token equations `fTok Γ_D Γ_E = Γ_D` and `gTok Γ_D Γ_E = Γ_E`, while `example_624` produces only the proper relations `Dsol ◁ Dsol + (Dsol × Esol)` and `Esol ◁ Dsol + Esol`. **Proof:** partial — the iteration from `({[]}, {[]})`, single-stage additivity, and the two monotonicity inclusions are the double fixed-point method for the token sets; the object-level step stops at `◁` 